WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.220
If BMP makes skin, what should the organizer make to turn ectoderm neural?

2
00:00:09.283 --> 00:00:10.943
Something that blocks BMP.

3
00:00:11.843 --> 00:00:13.343
It makes at least three such proteins.

4
00:00:14.467 --> 00:00:16.047
First, where they come from.

5
00:00:17.007 --> 00:00:21.447
Chordin and Noggin are made early, at the blastula stage, in dorsal animal cells.

6
00:00:22.517 --> 00:00:25.477
So neural induction starts before gastrulation.

7
00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:31.570
Then the organizer and dorsal mesoderm turn inward and spread beneath the ectoderm.

8
00:00:32.510 --> 00:00:33.510
This is involution.

9
00:00:35.483 --> 00:00:38.123
The involuting mesoderm secretes all three.

10
00:00:38.903 --> 00:00:41.363
The ectoderm above it becomes the neural plate.

11
00:00:42.367 --> 00:00:46.727
Noggin was found in 1992 by William Smith and Richard Harland.

12
00:00:47.607 --> 00:00:50.267
It is a secreted protein made in the organizer.

13
00:00:51.367 --> 00:00:57.407
In 1993, Teresa Lamb, Harland and colleagues added Noggin protein to intact caps.

14
00:00:58.307 --> 00:00:59.367
What should the caps become?

15
00:01:05.417 --> 00:01:08.077
Neural tissue, without dorsal mesoderm.

16
00:01:09.057 --> 00:01:10.817
Noggin acted on the ectoderm directly.

17
00:01:14.417 --> 00:01:16.017
The markers were all anterior.

18
00:01:17.177 --> 00:01:18.177
Forebrain is neural.

19
00:01:18.997 --> 00:01:21.717
Cement gland, a mucus gland at the head end, is not.

20
00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:29.580
Chordin was cloned in 1994 by Yoshiki Sasai and colleagues, in Eddy De Robertis's

21
00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:30.580
laboratory.

22
00:01:31.250 --> 00:01:35.870
Both Noggin and Chordin bind BMP4 and keep it off its receptors.

23
00:01:36.983 --> 00:01:40.643
Follistatin was known as a protein that binds activin.

24
00:01:41.303 --> 00:01:44.343
It also neuralizes caps and binds some BMPs.

25
00:01:45.483 --> 00:01:46.543
It works differently.

26
00:01:47.483 --> 00:01:51.503
It can stay bound to BMP as BMP docks on its receptor.

27
00:01:52.303 --> 00:01:53.863
That complex does not signal.

28
00:01:54.883 --> 00:01:56.963
None of the three needs to enter a cell.

29
00:01:57.823 --> 00:01:59.843
Each grabs BMP outside the cell.

30
00:02:00.817 --> 00:02:06.237
In the crystal structure, Noggin clasps a BMP dimer and covers the faces the

31
00:02:06.237 --> 00:02:07.257
receptors would bind.

32
00:02:08.317 --> 00:02:09.937
Chordin has a further twist.

33
00:02:10.837 --> 00:02:15.277
In the shuttling model, BMP bound to Chordin travels toward the ventral side.

34
00:02:16.277 --> 00:02:19.557
There, Tolloid proteases cut Chordin and release the BMP.

35
00:02:20.650 --> 00:02:22.610
Caps show what the catchers can do.

36
00:02:22.610 --> 00:02:24.590
Does the embryo need them?

37
00:02:25.483 --> 00:02:31.103
In 2005, Khokha and colleagues depleted Chordin, Noggin and Follistatin together

38
00:02:31.103 --> 00:02:32.183
from frog embryos.

39
00:02:33.263 --> 00:02:34.683
What happens to the neural plate?

40
00:02:40.667 --> 00:02:44.907
The embryos lost their dorsal structures, the neural plate among them.

41
00:02:45.587 --> 00:02:48.947
With all three catchers gone, BMP signaling stays high.

42
00:02:52.417 --> 00:02:53.877
The reverse test agreed.

43
00:02:55.117 --> 00:03:00.077
Reversade's group removed three BMPs from embryos whose organizer signals were

44
00:03:00.077 --> 00:03:03.437
blocked. The embryos formed massive amounts of brain.

45
00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:09.950
Early FGF signaling is also needed in the frog, in addition to blocking BMP.

46
00:03:11.010 --> 00:03:13.130
It can turn the dial down through MAPK.

47
00:03:13.910 --> 00:03:14.910
It also does more.

48
00:03:17.383 --> 00:03:21.723
The takeaway: the frog embryo needs the catchers to keep the dial down.

49
00:03:22.503 --> 00:03:24.463
And FGF adds a second input.
